From ad1078c09a7be3570d2ca98d7c3cc65c68604687 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:53:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] m68k: add the LiteX machine

A 68030-class CPU in a SoC built with LiteX
(https://github.com/enjoy-digital/litex), for instance RD68031, an
MC68030 soft core, and its RD68884 FPU, on an FPGA.

The SoC builder places its cores; the boot loader, built against the
SoC's generated headers, passes where they are as bootinfo records
(bootinfo-litex.h): no address is built into the kernel.

- The CPU's interrupt controller: every SoC interrupt line on one
  autovectored level, read from a pending and a mask CSR; a chained
  handler gives line n IRQ_USER + n.
- LiteX's Timer as the clock event device (one-shot and periodic), its
  64-bit uptime counter as the clock source.
- LiteUART as a platform device, and its earlycon.
- The CSR window (0xf0000000 up, above KMAP) mapped through TT1,
  uncached, as virt does for 0xff000000; ioremap() returns addresses
  there unchanged.
- Reset through the SoC controller.

The machine selects OF without using a device tree, so that
SERIAL_LITEUART (which depends on OF) can be built; it is probed as a
plain platform device.

Signed-off-by: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
